{%- comment -%}
  Scroll-linked image sequence on a canvas.

  This section has no counterpart in the React app and no counterpart in the
  design system. It was asked for directly, in the terms Apple's product pages
  use: preload a frame sequence, pin a canvas, map scroll position to a frame
  index, and draw frame by frame as the reader scrolls. The driver is
  assets/scroll-sequence.js with GSAP + ScrollTrigger; everything below is the
  markup and the data it reads.

  THE ARTWORK. 240 frames of a silk ribbon crossing a linen ground, which
  resolves into the polaroid wall the hero also leads with. Source frames 1-19
  and 234-240 are measurably static (mean absolute difference under 0.35 of 255)
  and were dropped. What ships is 54 frames sampled UNEVENLY out of the
  remaining 214, plus the poster:

    - every 5th source frame from 20 to 195 — the slow drift
    - every 2nd source frame from 197 to 233 — the reveal, which moves about
      twice as fast per frame as anything before it

  Scroll maps to the ARRAY index, not to the source timeline, so the reveal ends
  up with a third of the scroll distance for a sixth of the original running
  time. That is deliberate: the reveal is the only part with edges in it, and at
  even pacing it went past in a few hundred pixels.

  FEWER FRAMES THAN IT LOOKS. The driver crossfades between the two frames
  either side of the scroll position, so 54 frames read as continuous rather
  than as 54 steps. A 50/50 blend of two frames four apart was checked against a
  real frame from between them and is indistinguishable — silk has no hard edges
  to ghost. Without the crossfade this sequence would need roughly three times
  the frames, which at this material's compression cost is not affordable.

  WHY IT COSTS WHAT IT COSTS. Every frame is a full field of fine linen weave,
  which is the worst case for any image codec — there is no flat area anywhere to
  spend nothing on. Measured: 98KB per frame at 1120x630 webp q62, against about
  20KB for a frame of a product on white at the same size. So:

    - 54 frames + poster = 5.6MB, and there is no encoder setting that makes
      that 1MB. Dropping to 960px wide buys 40% and costs visible sharpness;
      going up to 1920px native costs 9.9MB.
    - A phone loads every 2nd frame instead — 28 frames, 2.9MB — decided once at
      init from the viewport width, never re-decided on resize, because
      re-deciding means downloading the other half.
    - Nothing loads at all until the section is within two viewports, and
      nothing loads on a metered or 2G connection, or under reduced motion.
      Those readers get the poster, which is the resolved last frame.

  THE LAST FRAME IS THE POSTER, and that is not a shortcut. The sequence is
  1120x630, which on a wide screen at 2x is about a 2.5x upscale — invisible on
  drifting silk, visible on the polaroids, which are photographs with edges. So
  the 55th and final entry in the frame list is sequence-poster.webp at 1600x900,
  the same composition at a quality the resting image deserves. The crossfade
  into it sharpens as the scrub finishes; mixing two resolutions in one dissolve
  cannot be seen because it is the same picture.

  THE FALLBACK IS THE DEFAULT STATE, which is why there is no `scripting: none`
  block for this one. The poster ships visible and the canvas ships hidden; the
  driver reveals the canvas and hides the poster only once every frame has
  decoded. Script blocked, script failed, reduced motion, save-data, 2G, GSAP
  404 — every one of those paths ends with a full-bleed photograph at the
  section's final size and no pinning, because the pin is created by
  ScrollTrigger and ScrollTrigger never runs. There is nothing to undo.

  ACCESSIBILITY. The canvas carries `role="img"` and the poster's alt text, so
  the sequence announces as one image rather than as nothing. It is an `h2` and
  the page's `h1` stays in the hero. The overlay is optional: with all three copy
  settings blank the whole card is omitted rather than drawing three placeholders
  over the artwork — the one place in this theme where a blank setting hides
  something instead of reserving space for it, because the artwork is the
  content here and the copy is the decoration, not the other way round.

  NOT GIVEN `data-reveal`, and not given `content-visibility`. The first
  animates opacity, which makes a stacking context and would fight the pin; the
  second hides the section from layout, and ScrollTrigger has to measure it.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  # The frame count is the shipped asset set, not a merchant setting: a wrong
  # number here is 54 requests for files that do not exist. Frames are
  # sequence-001.webp .. sequence-054.webp, and the poster is the 55th entry.
  assign frame_count = 54

  assign alt = section.settings.alt
  assign has_copy = false
  if section.settings.eyebrow != blank or section.settings.heading != blank or section.settings.body != blank
    assign has_copy = true
  endif
-%}

<section
  class="mt-section"
  data-sequence
  data-sequence-length="{{ section.settings.scroll_length }}"
  {{ section.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <div data-sequence-stage class="relative h-[100svh] w-full overflow-hidden bg-canvas">
    {%- comment -%}
      The poster is the resting state, so it is a real <img> with real alt text
      and `fetchpriority="low"` — it is well below the fold and must never
      compete with the hero for the first paint.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <img
      data-sequence-poster
      src="{{ 'sequence-poster.webp' | asset_url }}"
      alt="{{ alt | escape }}"
      width="1600"
      height="900"
      loading="lazy"
      fetchpriority="low"
      decoding="async"
      class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover"
    >

    {%- comment -%}
      No width/height: the driver sets the backing store from the stage's own box
      and the device pixel ratio, capped at 2x and 2600px, and re-sets it on
      refresh. Sizing it here would only be overwritten.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <canvas
      data-sequence-canvas
      role="img"
      aria-label="{{ alt | escape }}"
      class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full"
    ></canvas>

    {%- if has_copy -%}
      <div class="absolute inset-0 flex items-end md:items-center">
        <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-content px-gutter pb-12 md:pb-0">
          <div class="flex max-w-130 flex-col gap-4 bg-canvas/92 p-6 md:gap-5 md:px-11 md:py-12">
            <span class="text-micro uppercase tracking-nav text-ink-subtle">
              {% render 'copy', value: section.settings.eyebrow, label: 'eyebrow', inline: true %}
            </span>

            <h2 class="text-h1 leading-h2 tracking-display">
              {% render 'copy', value: section.settings.heading, label: 'heading' %}
            </h2>

            <p class="text-body leading-body text-ink-muted">
              {% render 'copy', value: section.settings.body, label: 'body', lines: 2 %}
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Every URL is emitted rather than one URL with the number substituted in
    script, because `asset_url` appends a per-file `?v=` hash — substituting the
    number would ask the CDN for frame 42 under frame 1's cache key. The list is
    about 6KB of highly repetitive text, which is almost nothing once gzipped.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script type="application/json" data-sequence-frames>
    [
      {%- for i in (1..frame_count) -%}
        {%- assign padded = i | prepend: '00' | slice: -3, 3 -%}
        {%- assign file = 'sequence-' | append: padded | append: '.webp' -%}
        "{{ file | asset_url }}",
      {%- endfor -%}
      "{{ 'sequence-poster.webp' | asset_url }}"
    ]
  </script>
</section>

{%- comment -%}
  GSAP is vendored as a theme asset rather than pulled from a CDN, and loaded
  here rather than in theme.liquid, so a page without this section pays none of
  its 117KB. `defer` keeps all three in document order and after parsing, which
  is the order the driver needs: it reads window.gsap and window.ScrollTrigger
  and returns quietly if either is missing.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script src="{{ 'gsap.min.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
<script src="{{ 'gsap-scrolltrigger.min.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
<script src="{{ 'scroll-sequence.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Scroll sequence",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "A pinned canvas that plays a 55-frame sequence as the reader scrolls. The frames ship with the theme and are not editable here. Readers on a metered or 2G connection, and readers who ask for reduced motion, see the last frame as a still photograph instead — so this section has to make sense as a single image too."
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "scroll_length",
      "label": "Scroll distance",
      "info": "How many screens of scrolling the sequence is spread over. Lower is faster and choppier; higher means more scrolling before the page moves on.",
      "min": 2,
      "max": 6,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "x",
      "default": 3
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "alt",
      "label": "Describe the sequence",
      "info": "Read aloud by screen readers, and the alt text of the still fallback. Describe what is shown, not that it animates."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Overlay"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Optional. Leave all three blank and no card is drawn — the artwork stands on its own."
    },
    { "type": "text", "id": "eyebrow", "label": "Eyebrow" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading" },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "body", "label": "Body" }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Scroll sequence"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
